The most likely diagnosis is primary biliary cholangitis (PBC). This condition typically affects middle-aged women and presents with pruritus, fatigue, and jaundice. Laboratory findings show a cholestatic pattern with elevated alkaline phosphatase and positive antimitochondrial antibodies (AMA). Liver biopsy reveals chronic nonsuppurative destruction of small intrahepatic bile ducts, which is characteristic of PBC.

Primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) usually involves larger bile ducts and is associated with inflammatory bowel disease; it is AMA-negative. Autoimmune hepatitis presents with elevated transaminases and positive ANA or anti-smooth muscle antibodies, not AMA. Choledocholithiasis causes obstructive jaundice and pain but lacks AMA positivity and the specific biopsy findings. Wilson disease involves copper accumulation and presents with neurological or hepatic symptoms in younger individuals, along with Kayser-Fleischer rings and low ceruloplasmin.

